  # ── The boat ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # A Nordic clinker boat with one square sail on a braced yard, generated from
  # nothing by Assets/Models/DriftBoat/build_drift_boat.py. Read that folder's
  # README.md for the model's contract with this scene.
  #
  # EVERY NUMBER BELOW THAT DESCRIBES THE HULL COMES OUT OF THAT SCRIPT: it floats
  # the drawn hull at this body's mass, measures it, and writes the component
  # values into the "physics" block of DriftBoat.rig.json.
  # DriftBoatDelivery.TheSceneIsBuiltFromTheGeneratedRig fails if this scene and
  # that file disagree, so after regenerating the boat this block is re-stamped
  # rather than re-tuned.
  #
  # SINCE #899 THIS IS A SAILING BOAT. The wind drives it (SailComponent below);
  # BoatComponent's thrust is an auxiliary for getting off a lee shore and out
  # of a flat calm, and on this boat it is a SWEEP, not an engine: the steering
  # oar sculled over the quarter. Read the sail block before re-tuning anything
  # here — hull drag and sail area are one ratio, not two independent numbers.
  #
  # THE HULL NUMBERS, and how each is derived:
  #
  #   * MASS 2600 kg is the game's number and the hull was drawn to carry it:
  #     at that displacement she floats 0.65 m deep with 0.62 m of freeboard
  #     amidships. The model's y = 0 IS that waterline and its z = 0 is the
  #     centre of buoyancy. This entity's origin is neither: the probe box
  #     below is centred on it and floats it 0.058 m above the water, so the
  #     hull mesh hangs that far below it.
  #   * THE COLLIDER (2 x 1.28 x 6 m) is the hull as a box: her waterline
  #     beam and length, keel to gunwale, offset down to where the hull is. It
  #     is what touches bottom when she grounds, and BoatWakeSystem sizes the
  #     wake and the flattened footprint from it. Jolt takes the body's CENTRE
  #     OF MASS and inertia from this box, so the centre of mass is the middle
  #     of the hull, 2 cm from her waterline, and 0.076 m below this origin.
  #     Every offset below (thrust point, centre of effort, probes) is measured
  #     from the ORIGIN; the forces act there and turn her about that centre.
  #   * THE PROBE BOX is BuoyancyComponent's submerged-hull model; see the note
  #     on ProbeExtents.
  #   * Forward damping is NOT ForwardDrag alone. BuoyancyComponent's own
  #     LinearDrag damps the same velocity, scaled by the submerged fraction, so
  #     the effective coefficient is
  #         ForwardDrag * hullImmersion + LinearDrag * submergedFraction
  #       = 0.12 * 1 + 0.355 * 0.2107 = 0.195 per second.
  #     That sum is the hull's half of the #899 ratio and it is the same number
  #     the previous hull had: a hull held back any harder cannot be moved by a
  #     sail, because the wind can only ever supply a few kN and supplies them
  #     as a force that FALLS as the boat speeds up.
  #   * Steady turn rate at full rudder is MaxRudderTorque / (YawDrag * mass) =
  #     7480 / (3.05 * 2600) = 0.94 rad/s, before DriftBoatController trims
  #     the rudder command back at speed. The rudder is the only control that
  #     still works with no wind at all.
  #
  # LinearDrag / AngularDrag on the rigidbody are ZERO on purpose. Buoyancy and
  # the hull model own all the damping; a second global damper on top of them
  # deadens the wave response the buoyancy probes exist to produce.
  - Entity: 879000000000020
    TagComponent:
      Tag: Boat
    TransformComponent:
      # Authored AT HER FLOATING HEIGHT: Y is how far this origin rides above
      # the sea, so the scene before Play shows her on her waterline and the
      # first physics tick has nothing to settle.
      Translation: [0, 0.058, -250]
      Rotation: [0, 0, 0]
      Scale: [1, 1, 1]
    Rigidbody3DComponent:
      BodyType: 1
      Mass: 2600
      LinearDrag: 0
      AngularDrag: 0
      DisableGravity: false
    BoxCollider3DComponent:
      HalfExtents: [1, 0.64, 3]
      # Down to the hull: the box's middle is the middle of the drawn hull, not
      # this origin. See THE COLLIDER above.
      Offset: [0, -0.076, 0]
    BuoyancyComponent:
      Enabled: true
      # THE SUBMERGED-HULL MODEL. BuoyancySystem floats this box on its eight
      # corners, each bottom corner carrying a share of the box's volume in
      # proportion to its depth over SubmergenceRamp. Three things follow:
      #
      #   * X (1.3) is the hull's real half-beam, and it is what sets the
      #     righting moment a loaded sail is fought back by. Narrower than the
      #     drawn hull and a rig big enough to move her rolls her clean over
      #     (#899 measured exactly that, upside down and floating).
      #   * Z (3.004) is half her waterline length.
      #   * Y (0.395) is NOT a height anyone measured. A box carrying this mass
      #     floats with its bottom corners
      #         d = mass * ramp / (4 * density * X * Y * Z) = 0.337 m
      #     under the sea. Rolled a little, those corners right her with
      #     weight * X^2 / d per radian, less weight times the height of the
      #     centre of mass above them, so a taller box is a stiffer one. Y is
      #     solved so that stiffness, divided by the height the sail's force
      #     acts at above the centre of mass (CentreOfEffortY, below), is the
      #     62 kN per radian the game was tuned at: she lies over as far in a
      #     given wind as she always has.
      #
      # The origin therefore floats Y - d = 0.058 m above the water (the hull mesh
      # hangs that far down), and BoatSystem takes the keel's depth from Y.
      ProbeExtents: [1.3, 0.395, 3.004]
      FluidDensity: 1000
      BuoyancyScale: 1
      # Vertical bobbing damper — but it damps SURGE too (BuoyancySystem applies
      # it to the whole velocity vector, scaled by the submerged fraction). The
      # product of the two is what the hull feels, and 0.355 is the value that
      # keeps that product where #899 tuned it for this box's wet fraction.
      LinearDrag: 0.355
      # Rocking damper. This is the knob that decides whether the swell reads as
      # a boat working in a seaway or as a bath toy. Set so a roll dies at the
      # rate it did before: the box's roll inertia went up with the collider.
      AngularDrag: 2.72
      SubmergenceRamp: 0.8
    # The AUXILIARY: the sweep. 900 N against the hull's damping is about
    # 1.8 m/s — enough to claw off a lee shore or ghost out of a flat calm (the
    # Clear leg's 2 m/s wind only makes about 0.6 m/s under sail), and
    # deliberately slower than the same boat under sail in any real breeze. That
    # ordering is the whole point and is pinned by
    # SailTest's DriftSailingTest.TheEngineIsSlowerThanTheSail.
    BoatComponent:
      Enabled: true
      MaxThrust: 900
      # The thrust point is the foot of the rudder blade, at its station.
      # BoatSystem also asks whether THIS point is under water before it lets
      # the rudder bite, which for a steering oar is exactly the right question:
      # she steers until the blade is out of the water.
      ThrustOffsetZ: -2.55
      ThrustOffsetY: -0.78
      # With YawDrag below, sets the turn rate and the time the helm takes to
      # answer. Both were scaled by the same factor as the box's yaw inertia,
      # so a longer hull steers exactly as the shorter one did.
      MaxRudderTorque: 7480
      # Full rudder authority only from 7 m/s up. Below that the rudder is soft,
      # which is what stops the boat pivoting on the spot in the harbour and
      # gives the low-speed handling a distinct character from the high-speed.
      RudderAuthoritySpeed: 7
      # Lateral >> forward is the hull: it is what makes the boat TRACK through a
      # turn instead of sliding broadside. Dropping this toward the forward value
      # is how you would author a hovercraft.
      LateralDrag: 3.5
      # See the effective-damping note above. LATERAL drag towers over it: that
      # ratio IS the keel, and on a sailing boat the keel is what converts the
      # sail's side force into forward travel instead of letting the boat slide
      # off downwind.
      ForwardDrag: 0.12
      YawDrag: 3.05
      # The depth over which the hull's drag and the rudder's bite ramp in. Kept
      # at the same share of the box's resting immersion as before, so the hull
      # grips fully at rest and lets go as a wave lifts her.
      ImmersionDepth: 0.3
      ThrottleInput: 0
      SteerInput: 0
    # ── The rig ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # THIS is the propulsion. SailSystem reads the scene WindSettings at the
    # centre of effort, subtracts the hull's own velocity to get apparent wind,
    # braces the yard to the angle that makes the most forward drive, and
    # applies a flat-plate force NORMAL to the sail at the point below.
    #
    # Three of these numbers decide how the game plays:
    #
    #   MaxYardAngle is the SPEED GOVERNOR and the no-go zone in one. On a reach
    #     the drive goes to zero once the boat is fast enough that the apparent
    #     wind draws forward of the braced yard, which caps beam-reach speed at
    #     roughly the true wind speed — so Clear (2 m/s), Overcast (5) and Storm
    #     (14) are three genuinely different boats. 45 deg is a square rigger's
    #     brace and puts the no-go zone at about 55-60 deg either side of the
    #     wind: there is no sailing upwind, only tacking. Raise it and the boat
    #     points higher AND goes faster; both, together, always. On the drawn
    #     boat 45 deg is also where the yard comes up against the lee shrouds,
    #     which are led aft for exactly that reason.
    #   SailArea is a GAME number and so is the hull's ForwardDrag above — what
    #     was tuned is the RATIO of the two. The sail that is drawn is 12.6 m^2;
    #     an honest 12.6 m^2 on a hull this heavily damped would take four minutes
    #     to cross the bay. Change one of the pair and re-measure, never just one.
    #   CentreOfEffort is NOT cosmetic, and it is NOT the middle of the drawn
    #     sail. Its HEIGHT (1.85 m above the origin, 1.93 above the centre of
    #     mass) is the whole heel: the side force acts there and buoyancy fights
    #     it back. The drawn sail's centre is 2.82 m up, but SailArea is twenty
    #     times the canvas that is drawn, and at that height its force laid her
    #     over 1.7 times as far as the game was tuned for. Stiffening the box
    #     hides that only at small angles: the side force that lifts the
    #     windward bilge clear is weight * half-beam / this height and nothing
    #     else, and with the true height she spent 1.6 times as long past ten
    #     degrees in the storm (37% of a beam reach against 23%, six runs each).
    #     So the height is 1.48 half-beams, as it was on the hull before her,
    #     which keeps that force at 17.2 kN. It is still the cheapest lever
    #     if she ever lies over too far.
    #     Its Z (-0.5 m, abaft the centre of mass) is the weather helm — a
    #     loaded sail nudges the bow up into the wind, and a gust rounds her up
    #     instead of bearing her away. It is NOT the drawn sail's station (-0.2 m).
    #     BoatSystem resists leeway AT the centre of mass; a real hull under way
    #     resists it forward of that, and what turns a boat is the distance
    #     between the two. So Z is set to give the yaw acceleration per newton
    #     of side force the game was tuned with (lever / yaw inertia), which on
    #     this hull is the drawn sail over a centre of lateral resistance
    #     0.3 m (5% of the waterline) forward of the centre of mass.
    SailComponent:
      Enabled: true
      SailArea: 260
      AirDensity: 1.225
      MaxNormalCoefficient: 1.5
      MaxYardAngleDeg: 45
      # A yard takes a few seconds to come round. Fast enough that a tack is not
      # a wait, slow enough that it reads as being hauled rather than snapped.
      TrimRateDeg: 28
      CentreOfEffortY: 1.85
      CentreOfEffortZ: -0.5
      AutoTrim: true
      TrimInput: 0
      SailSetInput: 1
    LuaScriptComponent:
      ScriptFile: Scripts/LuaScripts/DriftBoatController.lua
    # Discovery loop (issue #881): the boat is the discoverer. DiscoverySystem
    # appends an island's UUID here the first time an active contact pairs
    # the boat with a DiscoverableComponent island's trigger volume.
    DiscoveredSetComponent:
      Discovered: []
    RelationshipComponent:
      ParentHandle: 0
      Children:
        - 879000000000021

  # ── Hull mesh ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # The hull, decks, mast, standing rigging and deck gear: one mesh on one
  # material (DriftBoatHull), at true scale. The model's y = 0 is its waterline,
  # and the Translation below is how far the body's origin floats above the
  # sea, so in Play the two waterlines meet.
  #
  # A MeshComponent, not a ModelComponent, because that is the path that has
  # levels of detail. The three levels are the generator's own (it rebuilds the
  # boat with fewer stations and drops what is too small to see, on the SAME
  # texture atlas), not a simplifier's: past 90 m the standing rigging is drawn
  # thicker on purpose, because a line under a pixel wide shimmers. Shadows are
  # always cast by level 0. In Play the chase camera is 9-14 m away and this is
  # always level 0; the far levels are for anything that looks at her from
  # across the water.
  - Entity: 879000000000021
    TagComponent:
      Tag: Boat Hull
    TransformComponent:
      Translation: [0, -0.058, 0]
      Rotation: [0, 0, 0]
      Scale: [1, 1, 1]
    MeshComponent:
      MeshSourceHandle: 18313841716185698139
    LODGroupComponent:
      Enabled: true
      Bias: 1
      Levels:
        - MeshHandle: 18313841716185698154
          MaxDistance: 30
          TriangleCount: 30860
        - MeshHandle: 18313841716185698155
          MaxDistance: 90
          TriangleCount: 8961
        - MeshHandle: 18313841716185698156
          MaxDistance: 100000
          TriangleCount: 2270
    RelationshipComponent:
      ParentHandle: 879000000000020
      Children:
        - 879000000000022
        - 879000000000023
        - 879000000000041
        - 879000000000042
        - 879000000000043
        - 879000000000044

  # ── The sail ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # The yard and the sail, on their own entity so they can point somewhere
  # (#899). DriftBoatSail.gltf is origined ON THE MAST AXIS, so this entity's
  # Y ROTATION IS THE YARD ANGLE: DriftBoatController copies
  # SailComponent::yardAngle onto it every tick and the sail braces round the
  # mast. The translation is the mast's position in the hull.
  #
  # Rotation is authored at zero — the yard squared, which is the trim for a dead
  # run — so the first frame before any script tick looks correct.
  #
  # THE CLOTH IS ALIVE. The sail's files carry 10 morph targets (Slack, Hard, Aback, Furled,
  # three phases of a luffing wave and three of a finer ripple), and
  # DriftBoatController blends them every tick from what the wind is doing to
  # her: how hard it presses, on which face, how much of her is set. The
  # MorphTargetComponent below is what lets the engine deform the mesh; its
  # weights are the script's, so it is authored empty and what is drawn before
  # Play is the sail full of a moderate breeze. The two "Furled" markers are where
  # the clews are with the sail gathered right up to the yard, so the sheets can
  # follow the clews as she is shortened.
  - Entity: 879000000000022
    TagComponent:
      Tag: Boat Sail
    TransformComponent:
      Translation: [0, 0, -0.5]
      Rotation: [0, 0, 0]
      Scale: [1, 1, 1]
    MeshComponent:
      MeshSourceHandle: 18313841716185698149
    LODGroupComponent:
      Enabled: true
      Bias: 1
      Levels:
        - MeshHandle: 18313841716185698157
          MaxDistance: 30
          TriangleCount: 15492
        - MeshHandle: 18313841716185698158
          MaxDistance: 90
          TriangleCount: 2766
        - MeshHandle: 18313841716185698159
          MaxDistance: 100000
          TriangleCount: 360
    MorphTargetComponent:
      Weights: {}
    RelationshipComponent:
      ParentHandle: 879000000000021
      Children:
        - 879000000000045
        - 879000000000046
        - 879000000000047
        - 879000000000048
        - 879000000000049
        - 879000000000050

  # ── The rudder ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # A side rudder on the starboard quarter (-X: this engine's starboard for a
  # +Z bow). Its file is origined on the shaft at the gunwale, so the entity
  # carries two rotations with different owners:
  #
  #   * Z is the CANT (16.6 deg), authored here: it lays the shaft along the
  #     hull's side, foot tucked in toward the keel;
  #   * Y is the HELM, written every tick by DriftBoatController (up to
  #     28 deg). The engine applies Y before Z, so the blade turns about its
  #     own canted shaft rather than about the vertical.
  - Entity: 879000000000023
    TagComponent:
      Tag: Boat Rudder
    TransformComponent:
      Translation: [-0.724, 0.917, -2.55]
      Rotation: [0, 0, 0.28938]
      Scale: [1, 1, 1]
    MeshComponent:
      MeshSourceHandle: 18313841716185698147
    LODGroupComponent:
      Enabled: true
      Bias: 1
      Levels:
        - MeshHandle: 18313841716185698160
          MaxDistance: 30
          TriangleCount: 646
        - MeshHandle: 18313841716185698161
          MaxDistance: 100000
          TriangleCount: 170
    RelationshipComponent:
      ParentHandle: 879000000000021
      Children: []

  # ── Sheets and braces ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # The running rigging that follows the yard. Each is DriftBoatLine.gltf — one
  # metre of rope along its own +Z — placed at its LEAD on the gunwale, and
  # DriftBoatController aims and stretches it every tick to the marker entity
  # under the sail that is its far end (a clew for a sheet, a yard arm for a
  # brace). So each Translation here is a lead, and Rotation / Scale are only
  # the pose for the yard squared, which is what the scene shows before Play.
  - Entity: 879000000000041
